  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale: We'll use an ensemble surrogate optimization approach based on LP-based descent with constraint generation and adaptive length variation. The central idea is to improve the previous program by focusing optimization on the most promising seeds (parent sequence, uniform, and window-type sequences) and then using perturbations to explore the neighborhood. We'll prioritize computational speed by focusing on length halving (which tends to reduce the objective faster) and use a time-based budgeting system to limit evaluations. The key innovation is to adaptively adjust the optimization path based on directional gradients rather than fixed time limits.\n\n2. Mathematical specification: \n   - Variables: normalized convex combination sequences of length n, represented by vectors in \u211d\u207a\u207f\n   - Objective: min_{a} (2 * n * max(convolve(a)) ) / (sum(a))\u00b2\n   - Constraints: a must be nonnegative and sum(a) \u2265 0.01\n   - For each seed: compute the auto-convolution peak and use the full linear convolution via FFT for gradient estimation\n   - The simplex projection and L1/direction normalization maintain feasibility throughout iterations\n\n3. Implementation procedure: Steps\n   Step 1: Parametrize and validate the input\n        - n0 = 1152 (parent length)\n        - Generate three seeds: parent, round(length)/.2 uniform, and flat_top_window(rdn)\n        - Sort along with the parent generation's n_base (1024, 1152, 1280...)\n        - Structure: seed initialization, autocorrelation computation, directional gradient generation, subsequent descent\n\n   Step 2: Run initial evaluation on parent and seeds\n        - Evaluate seed sequences with evaluate_sequence() \n        - Track lowest_initial_score and best_seed\n        - For seed evaluation: use only valid sequences (nonnegative, finite, sum\u22650.01)\n\n   Step 3: Set global deadline to 1800 seconds\n        - For each phase, check if the time budget is exceeded\n        - Time apportionment per-section:\n\n   Step 4: Descent+Polish kernel for the best_seed: \n        - reify the active set from the top constraints (unfolds from the previous program's constraint generation)\n        - For each iteration, solve a binary-convolution constraint system with the simplex projection as fallback\n\n   Step 5: Multi-peak adaptive gradient descent\n        - For each significant peak in the convolution, generate gradient information\n        - Line search uses exact gradients (not surrogate) and proper scaling\n\n   Step 6: Progressive refinement with adaptive length\n        - Eight points from the previous program's phases, optimized with increased aggressiveness\n\n   Step 7: Time-bounded fallback with orthogonal search space\n        - Minimum fallback: unit-sum sequence of length n_best\n        - Check after each stage if any improvement occurred\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Primary seeds: parent's length, and comparable lengths (n0, n0*1.1, n0//1.1)\n   - Halving vs. doubling: implement dynamic length adaptation via golden-section search sampling\n   - Bandwidth parameter: keep the simplex dimension consistent with the seed length\n   - Restart frequency: every 500 seconds with new randomization\n   - Step size: controlled by parameterized line-search to maintain true gradient direction\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n   - Total time required: 60 minutes (vs. previous 120 minutes)\n   - Peak budget: 100 seconds per evaluation\n   - Exit criteria: minimum dp/improvement cycle or time deadline\n   - Fallback: simply the parent sequence and its evaluations\n   - This plan explicitly separates gradient computation and optimization updates and leverages FFT speed-ups\n\n6. Validation: \n   - Each seed is evaluated only once\n   - The final candidate is re-evaluated with the same function\n   - Individual steps are optimized with the fallback protection from the previous program\n\nAll steps above are to be implemented using the helper functions specified in the previous program and our own adapted evaluation function, ensuring the optimizer maintains the step characteristics but improves the objective by leveraging better gradient estimation. The full code is structured to run quickly within the 1800 seconds while finding better results than the previous program.",
